  <img decoding="async" width="696" height="392" src="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/fed-768x432-1.jpg" class="attachment-medium_large size-medium_large wp-post-image" alt="Federal Reserve Bank Officials: Cryptocurrency Sell-off Not a Systematic Concern, Does Not Affect Fed Policy" loading="lazy" style="float:left; margin:0 15px 15px 0;" srcset="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/fed-768x432-1.jpg 768w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/fed-300x169.jpg 300w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/fed-1024x576.jpg 1024w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/fed-696x392.jpg 696w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/fed-1068x601.jpg 1068w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/fed-747x420.jpg 747w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/fed-190x107.jpg 190w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/fed-380x214.jpg 380w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/fed-760x428.jpg 760w, https://news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/fed.jpg 1280w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 696px) 100vw, 696px" /></p> 
  
  <p>
    <strong>Cryptocurrency sell-off is not a systematic concern for the Fed and does not affect Fed’s policies, two Federal Reserve Bank presidents said Wednesday. “We are all quite aware that crypto can be very volatile,” said the president of the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis.</strong>
  </p>
  
  <h2 style="text-align: center;">
    Crypto Sell-off Does Not Change Fed Policy
  </h2>
  
  <p>
    Two Federal Reserve Bank presidents have commented on the steep sell-off in cryptocurrencies Wednesday. St. Louis Federal Reserve President James Bullard, who firmly believes that cryptocurrency poses no threat to the U.S. dollar, said:
  </p>
  
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      By itself I don’t see that as a systemic concern at this point. We are all quite aware that crypto can be very volatile.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  
  <p>
    Bullard said in February that whether “bitcoin price goes up or down” it “doesn’t really affect” the Fed policies. He added that the cryptocurrency is no threat to the USD because “investors want a safe haven. They want a stable store of value, and then they want to conduct their investments in that currency.”
  </p>
  
  <p>
    Another Federal Reserve Bank president who commented on the cryptocurrency sell-off Wednesday was Atlanta Fed President Raphael Bostic. Making a similar statement during an interview with Bloomberg TV, he said:
  </p>
  
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      There’s a lot of volatility in it, but right now it’s not at a scale and it doesn’t have a reach into the economy that has systemic implications for us.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  
  <p style="text-align: center;">
    <!-- growjs zone placement 31 -->
    
    <ins> <ins class="growjs-placement"></ins> </ins> <!-- end of growjs zone placement -->
  </p>
  
  <p>
    “It’s not something I really incorporate very much into how I think about where our policy should be,” he affirmed.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    Bitcoin’s price briefly fell below $30K on Bitfinex early Wednesday morning but has since recovered. The price of BTC is currently $40,361 based on data from markets.Bitcoin.com.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    <em><strong>What do you think about what the two Federal Reserve Bank presidents said about cryptocurrency? Let us know in the comments section below.</strong></em>
  </p>
